---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---
<Base title="Padel guide: history, rules and game formats | Padel Culture" description="Where padel came from, how the game is played, and how Americano, Mexicano, King of the Court and tournaments work.">
  <Header />
  <main class="wrap doc">
    <p class="eyebrow">Padel guide</p>
    <h1>New to padel? Start here.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Where the game came from, how it's played, and how each of our game formats works.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#history">History</a>
      <a href="#basics">The basics</a>
      <a href="#americano">Americano</a>
      <a href="#mexicano">Mexicano</a>
      <a href="#king">King of the Court</a>
      <a href="#tournaments">Tournaments</a>
      <a href="#words">Padel words</a>
    </nav>

    <section id="history">
      <h2>A short history of padel</h2>
      <p>Padel was invented in 1969 in Acapulco, Mexico. Enrique Corcuera didn't have room for a full tennis court at home, so he built a smaller one and closed it in with walls. The ball stayed in play off the walls, and a new game came out of it.</p>
      <p>His friend Alfonso de Hohenlohe took the idea to Spain and built the first courts at the Marbella Club in 1974. A year later Julio Menditeguy brought it to Argentina. Those two countries turned it into a national pastime, and they still produce most of the world's top players.</p>
      <p>The International Padel Federation was founded in 1991 and the first World Championships followed in 1992. Padel is now one of the fastest-growing sports in the world, and courts are opening across India.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="basics">
      <h2>The basics</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Always doubles.</strong> Two against two, on a 20 m by 10 m court enclosed by glass and mesh.</li>
        <li><strong>Underarm serve.</strong> Bounce the ball and hit it at or below waist height, diagonally into the opposite box.</li>
        <li><strong>The walls are in play.</strong> The ball must bounce on the ground first. After that it can hit the glass and you can still return it.</li>
        <li><strong>Tennis scoring.</strong> 15, 30, 40, game. Six games win a set. Social formats like Americano count every point instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Easy to start.</strong> The racket is short and solid, the court is small, and rallies last. Most people are playing real points within their first session.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="americano">
      <h2>How an Americano works</h2>
      <p class="tag">Best for: meeting people, mixed groups, your first game</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Come on your own.</strong> You don't need a partner. Everyone is paired up by the organiser.</li>
        <li><strong>Play short rounds.</strong> Each round is played to a fixed number of points, usually 21 or 24 in total. If the round ends 14–10, you take 14 points and your opponents take 10.</li>
        <li><strong>Switch partners every round.</strong> By the end you've played with and against most of the group.</li>
        <li><strong>Your points are your own.</strong> Every point your pair wins goes on your individual tally. The player with the most points at the end wins.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="mexicano">
      <h2>How a Mexicano works</h2>
      <p class="tag">Best for: closer matches, groups with a wide range of levels</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Round one is random.</strong> Scoring is the same as an Americano: fixed points per round, and every point counts for you.</li>
        <li><strong>After that, the leaderboard picks the matches.</strong> Players are grouped by their current standing. On each court, 1st and 3rd play against 2nd and 4th.</li>
        <li><strong>The games get tighter.</strong> You keep meeting players who are scoring like you, so the last rounds are usually the closest.</li>
        <li><strong>Most points wins.</strong> The same individual tally decides the night.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="king">
      <h2>How King of the Court works</h2>
      <p class="tag">Best for: fast, competitive sessions</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Courts are ranked.</strong> One court is the King's court, and the rest line up below it.</li>
        <li><strong>Rounds are short and timed.</strong> Usually 8 to 12 minutes. Whoever is ahead at the whistle wins the round.</li>
        <li><strong>Winners move up, losers move down.</strong> Win and you climb one court towards the King's court. Lose and you drop one.</li>
        <li><strong>Partners split.</strong> In the social version you change partners after every round, so nobody stays on top with the same teammate.</li>
        <li><strong>Hold the top court.</strong> The winner is whoever finishes on the King's court, or wins the most rounds there.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="tournaments">
      <h2>How our tournaments work</h2>
      <p class="tag">Best for: fixed pairs who want a proper competition</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Enter as a pair.</strong> You keep the same partner throughout.</li>
        <li><strong>Group stage first.</strong> Every pair plays a few matches in a group, so nobody goes home after one game.</li>
        <li><strong>Then knockouts.</strong> The top pairs from each group go through to quarter-finals, semi-finals and a final.</li>
        <li><strong>Proper scoring.</strong> Matches use games and sets. At deuce we usually play a golden point: one point decides the game.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="words">
      <h2>Padel words you'll hear</h2>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Bandeja</dt><dd>A controlled, sliced overhead. You play it to keep your place at the net, not to win the point outright.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Víbora</dt><dd>A more aggressive overhead hit with side spin, so the ball skids off the glass.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Por tres</dt><dd>A smash that bounces and flies out over the 3-metre side wall. Por cuatro goes out over the 4-metre back wall.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Chiquita</dt><dd>A soft, low shot at the feet of the players at the net.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Golden point</dt><dd>One deciding point at deuce. The receiving pair chooses which side to receive on.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <p class="cta"><a class="btn btn-solid" href="/#games">See upcoming games</a> <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/join">Get your rating</a></p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .doc { max-width: 46rem; display: grid; gap: 1.2rem; padding-block: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5rem); }
  .doc h1 { font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 3.8rem); }
  .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--mist); }
  .toc { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .toc a { padding: 0.4rem 0.9rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 999px; font-size: 0.85rem; text-decoration: none; color: var(--chalk); background: var(--pine); }
  .toc a:hover { background: var(--moss); }
  section { display: grid; gap: 0.8rem; padding-top: 1.6rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  section h2 { font-size: 2rem; }
  section p, section li, dd { color: var(--mist); line-height: 1.6; }
  section strong, dt { color: var(--chalk); }
  .tag { font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--olive); font-weight: 600; }
  ol, ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.3rem; display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; }
  dl { margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 0.8rem; }
  dt { font-weight: 600; }
  dd { margin: 0; }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.8rem; padding-top: 1rem; }
</style>
